hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The run failed for a technical reason, not because of the rule. `collections` is an allowed module, but the environment does not pre-import it, so `transform` crashed before producing any output. The fix is to import it inside the function. Nothing else changes.\n\nThe rule itself stays as described:\n\n**What stays fixed.** The two colour-2 brackets do not change. Each bracket's spine is its fullest column or row:\n- In pairs 1 and 3 the spines are vertical, at x=3 and x=10.\n- In pair 2 and the test they are horizontal, at y=4 and y=11.\n\n**What moves.** Every other non-background cell is erased. It is then redrawn at its mirror image across the spine nearer to it: coordinate c becomes 2s − c. Shapes near one spine are flipped outside that bracket together.\n\n**Test input.**\n- The 4-cell bar in row 6 goes to row 2, and the 3-cell bar in row 7 goes to row 1, both mirrored across row 4.\n- The 3-cell bar in row 9 goes to row 13, mirrored across row 11.\n\n**Edit stages.**\n1. Erase the inner cells.\n2. Stamp the mirrored cells for each spine in turn.\n3. Emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    bcol = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])[\"colour\"]\n    brackets = [o for o in objs if o[\"colour\"] == bcol]\n    # orientation and spines\n    spines = []\n    vertical = None\n    for o in brackets:\n        ys = collections.Counter(y for y, x in o[\"cells\"])\n        xs = collections.Counter(x for y, x in o[\"cells\"])\n        cy, ny = ys.most_common(1)[0]\n        cx, nx = xs.most_common(1)[0]\n        if nx >= ny:\n            vertical = True\n            spines.append(cx)\n        else:\n            vertical = False\n            spines.append(cy)\n    movers = [(y, x) for y in range(g.shape[0]) for x in range(g.shape[1])\n              if g[y, x] != bg and g[y, x] != bcol]\n    out = g.copy()\n    for y, x in movers:\n        out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase the loose cells lying between the two brackets.\")\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for s in sorted(set(spines)):\n        placed = False\n        for y, x in movers:\n            c = x if vertical else y\n            near = min(spines, key=lambda t: abs(t - c))\n            if near != s:\n                continue\n            nc = 2 * s - c\n            ny, nx = (y, nc) if vertical else (nc, x)\n            if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W:\n                out[ny, nx] = g[y, x]\n                placed = True\n        if placed:\n            emit(out, f\"Mirror the cells nearest the spine at {'x' if vertical else 'y'}={s} to the outside of that bracket.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every inner cell reflected through its nearer bracket spine.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is then redrawn at its mirror image across the spine nearer to it: coordinate c becomes 2s − c."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The 4-cell bar in row 6 goes to row 2, and the 3-cell bar in row 7 goes to row 1, both mirrored across row 4."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The 3-cell bar in row 9 goes to row 13, mirrored across row 11."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Stamp the mirrored cells for each spine in turn."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"d8408d1470973850c55e799b6904ba346466174e82044d78a7b60f399d93a74f","task_sha256":"a5456ec9982d9cbf825fdb1328abfc422ed3e3706c0a7794f9413eb7f98744c4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
